{
  "requirement": "For a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer to apply the guidance of {cite}`requirements.cli.distributed_guidance`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation. For a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer to apply the guidance of `requirements.cli.distributed_guidance`. That guidance is the bundled skill's explanation of distributed requirements. The cited requirement is context only, so I did not reassess its tests.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_applies_the_distributed_guidance]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It reads the real bundled prompt through `review_prompt()`, which returns the `habeas-requirements` skill's `references/review-prompt.md`. It extracts the `## Criteria` section with `section`, which fails if the heading is missing. It then calls `assert_text`, which asserts that the section contains the phrase \"for a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, apply the Do and Don't lists in the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`\".\n\nThe assertion covers both parts of the obligation:\n- The condition: the phrase names the `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries.\n- The directive: the phrase tells the reviewer to apply the distributed guidance, and it points to that guidance file.\n\n`assert_text` uses `text.contains` and panics with a message when the phrase is missing, so the assertion is enforced. A prompt that dropped the directive, or moved it out of the Criteria section, would fail the test. The text names no other modes or boundary cases that need separate tests.",
